# B1 E0 P1 result, selective-policy audit, and leakage correction

Date: 2026-07-15

## Runtime result

- Experiment: `vea_eval_20260714_185828_860db0`
- Eval run: `0779527a-ccf1-49b4-ba6c-94481f8b6fb4`
- Job run: `ea2bc636-72f6-47ab-a625-935823548842`
- HARS monitor: `b1-e0-p1-promo250-7track-20260714-r1`
- Artifact: `/mnt/datasets/eval/b1_e0_natural_qa_promo250_7track_20260714/report.json`
- Terminal state: completed, 250 fact items from 50 episode clusters, 7 causal arms, TRT pass rate 1.0.

## Main metrics

| Arm | Accuracy | Episode-bootstrap 95% CI |
|---|---:|---:|
| raw | 0.640 | [0.584, 0.692] |
| empty | 0.672 | [0.608, 0.732] |
| oracle | 1.000 | [1.000, 1.000] |
| predicted, always on | 0.632 | [0.568, 0.692] |
| contradiction | 0.040 | [0.016, 0.068] |
| wrong episode bundle | 0.532 | [0.476, 0.588] |
| irrelevant bundle | 0.640 | [0.576, 0.700] |

Oracle lift was +0.360 [0.308, 0.416]. Always-on predicted lift was -0.008 [-0.096, 0.080]. This proves the typed text interface can causally control the consumer, but falsifies the always-on producer policy.

## Exploratory selector result

An episode-grouped five-fold out-of-fold selector analysis chose the confidence threshold on four folds and evaluated it on the held-out fold. Pooled result:

- coverage 0.436;
- producer precision 0.734;
- raw accuracy 0.640;
- selective-policy accuracy 0.720;
- lift +0.080, episode-bootstrap 95% CI [0.020, 0.140];
- all five held-out folds had positive lift (+0.04 to +0.10).

Artifact: `/mnt/datasets/eval/b1_e0_natural_qa_promo250_7track_20260714/selective_policy_cv.json`.

This is an exploratory signal only. The selector protocol was developed after inspecting this run and therefore requires a frozen calibration protocol plus a new untouched confirmatory set.

## Leakage correction

The P1 evaluation is not producer-untouched. The actual A2 checkpoints are Qwen36 `autohires_full1000` producers. Their fitting/model-selection episode sets overlap P1 evaluation by four episodes (three producer-train, one producer-val). The current strict two-camera pool also has no unused episodes relative to Phase-C plus P1: 267 Phase-C train episodes and 50 P1 episodes exhaust all 317.

Therefore P1 and its cross-validation must not be promoted as confirmatory evidence. They remain useful for interface causality and for designing the next frozen selector protocol.

## Required next gate

Do not rerun the old checkpoints and call the result confirmatory. The same-camera contract requires retraining both A2 producer probes on a new episode-disjoint partition, reserving separate selector-calibration and single-use confirmation episode sets. If that partition cannot satisfy all front+wrist embedding and raw-video requirements, the alternative front-only pool must be labeled a camera-domain generalization experiment, not a P2 replication.

## P2 disjoint retraining result

The sealed stratified split was materialized before retraining:

- producer train: 459 episodes;
- producer validation: 50 episodes;
- selector calibration: 50 strict two-camera episodes;
- sealed confirmation: 50 strict two-camera episodes;
- zero episode overlap; every configured fact head has positive examples in every atomic partition;
- encoder contract: `dinov3-large`, front+wrist precomputed embeddings;
- manifests: `training/configs/vea/evidence_bridge/splits/droid_hires_*_seed20260715/manifest.json`.

The first category attempt used the wrong historical base config (`balanced`, without the validated grid-position plus absent-cap recipe). It correctly reproduced the known absent-majority failure and was rejected:

- pipeline: `16d6c617-a1ae-45c7-9e58-9d76293746b8`;
- experiment: `vea_train_20260714_235506_f9deda`;
- result: failed target gate; `val/fact_class_1_recall=0.2778 < 0.35`.

The corrected run used the validated `gridpos_absentcap_cont` architecture but removed its contaminated continuation checkpoint and trained from scratch:

- pipeline: `37abd6f9-c390-48fc-bd91-20dcab664a68`;
- category experiment: `vea_train_20260715_000039_94b3d7`;
- terminal `last.ckpt` failed the target gate: `val/fact_class_1_recall=0.3215 < 0.35`, while validation accuracy was about 0.734;
- the run had already crossed the recall gate at step 50 (`0.4131`) and step 100 (`0.3945`), but those weights were not retained.

The independent A2.2.1 attribute producer was then tested from scratch on the same producer split:

- experiment: `vea_train_20260715_000342_fdfe6c`;
- job run: `f4c59eb5-cebd-4efd-a9d1-05c305c3ed0a`;
- terminal `last.ckpt` had `val/fact_acc=0.72715`, `val/fact_class_1_recall=0.34255 < 0.35`;
- step 100 had already crossed both frozen gates (`recall=0.4681`, `accuracy=0.6881`), and the early-stopper callback recorded a best recall of about `0.4793`.

Initial terminal verdict: neither late `last.ckpt` passed its configured promotion gate. Do not run selector calibration or touch the sealed confirmation set with these checkpoints.

Subsequent trajectory/config audit materially narrows that conclusion. These runs do **not** cleanly falsify the DINO representation:

- the old strict-hires A2 recipes used `dinov3-large-336`, while the new runs explicitly overrode the representation to `dinov3-large`;
- the hard recall metric uses argmax and is operating-point sensitive;
- category exceeded 0.35 recall at early validation points (`0.4131` at step 50 and `0.3945` at step 100);
- attribute crossed both frozen promotion gates at step 100 (`recall=0.4681`, `accuracy=0.6881`) and retained them at step 150 (`recall=0.4498`, `accuracy=0.7046`);
- the monitored `ModelCheckpoint` callback did not save any validation optimum: runtime state has `every_n_train_steps=0`, `every_n_epochs=0`, `best_model_score=null`, and an empty `best_model_path`; only late `last.ckpt` files survived after safety-snapshot pruning;
- the logged training recall is a macro-like unweighted average of per-task/per-channel hard-argmax recalls, while `PresenceProbePredictor` first averages channel logits and then applies argmax, so model selection and production use different decision paths;
- cross-entropy loss decreased monotonically as class-0 recall rose and class-1 recall fell or oscillated;
- train and validation positive recall were similarly low at termination, arguing against ordinary train/validation overfit;
- category class weights came from the old split, attribute used stale uniform weights, and both independently supervised scene labels on front and wrist heads even when the queried object may not be visible in one camera.

Corrected verdict: the claim that the clean recipes failed to reproduce is unsupported. Both runs produced gate-passing weights and then discarded them because checkpoint selection was disabled for intra-epoch validation. What failed is the persistence/selection protocol; representation absence remains unresolved and the encoder comparison is additionally confounded. The next gate is an offline producer-validation logit/PR/calibration audit of the surviving late checkpoints, followed by short clean-split reruns only after validation-optimum persistence and production-aligned metrics are fixed. The exact `dinov3-large-336` arm and producer-train-derived weights remain the first controlled reproduction. Calibration and sealed confirmation remain untouched.

## P2 producer recovery experiments

The checkpoint callback was repaired to save top-k weights on every validation event. The previous implementation hardcoded both checkpoint schedules off; runtime callback state therefore had `best_model_score=null`. The repaired callback now produced real `best-*` files and non-null best scores in every recovery run.

A CPU-only HARS eval runner was added for DROID presence probes. It evaluates only producer-val, follows production inference (`mean(channel logits) -> softmax/threshold`), and reports micro/macro confusion, precision, recall, specificity, AP versus prevalence, calibration error, and global/per-head threshold feasibility. Selector calibration and sealed confirmation were not opened.

